{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"lowcost-unsainted-wallaby","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context\n\n`tests/interfaces/store/test_event_append_index_lock.py::test_contended_index_lock_cleared_during_backoff_succeeds` failed on the `pytest (macos-latest, py3.13)` leg of the Gerrit Verified gate on TWO unrelated changes within the same hour on 2026-08-09:\n\n- change 1575 (ticket 81d5, rate-limit sweep test + adapters), run 31338947918\n- change 1574 (ticket cc99, Makefile/scripts typecheck), run 31338xxxxx — reported by that ticket's author as failing after its own Verified vote had already passed\n\nNeither change touches `src/rebar/_store`. On run 31338947918 the three Linux legs (py3.11, py3.12, py3.13) passed the identical tree; only macOS failed, and that leg took 23m24s against ~12m for each Linux leg.\n\nFailure mode:\n\n    rebar._store.event_append.StoreError: Error: git commit failed while holding lock:\n    fatal: unable to write new index file\n\n'unable to write new index file' is a filesystem/resource symptom (disk pressure or contention on the runner), not a lock-protocol defect — but the test is specifically the CONTENDED index-lock backoff case, so it is the most timing-sensitive test in the store suite and the first to break when the runner is slow.\n\nCost: a spurious `Verified -1` forces a `recheck` cycle (~25 min) on unrelated changes, and it trains authors to treat red CI as noise, which is how a real failure gets waved through.\n\n## Plan\n\nDetermine whether this is (a) genuine runner resource exhaustion, (b) a too-tight timing assumption in the test that only shows up when macOS is slow, or (c) a real narrow race in the backoff path that only a slow machine widens. Do NOT simply add a retry or mark it flaky before distinguishing (c) from (a)/(b) — (c) would be a genuine store bug.\n\nStart by correlating the macOS leg's wall-clock with its failure rate across recent runs, and check whether the test's backoff/timeout constants are absolute rather than scaled to observed machine speed.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The failure is classified as runner-resource, test-timing, or a real backoff race, with evidence from more than one run.\n- [ ] If timing: the test's assumptions are made robust to a slow runner without weakening what it proves about contended-lock recovery.\n- [ ] If a real race: a held-out test reproduces it deterministically and the store path is fixed.\n- [ ] The macOS py3.13 leg passes the store suite across at least 5 consecutive runs after the change.\n- [ ] If the root cause is runner resource exhaustion outside our control, that conclusion is recorded on the ticket with its evidence and the test is made resilient rather than deleted.","id":"50b1-e181-3716-482c","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Flaky: test_contended_index_lock_cleared_during_backoff_succeeds fails on macOS py3.13 CI under load"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786315841483718001,"uuid":"b994d732-19b7-4164-ac9f-ced550107189"}